{
  "summary": "Critiqued the converged dependency-tracking design across five areas: (1) the impl_stages poison/cancellation mechanism only covers planning tasks but not in-flight implementation tasks, leaving a race where a dependency rejection mid-implementation produces work built on stale assumptions; (2) the override field has no auto-clear policy when blocking dependencies later resolve, creating stale metadata that misleads both auto-mode and UI indicators; (3) the dependency_events 50-entry LRU cap has no defined behavior when all entries are unaddressed (many rejections, no re-planning yet), risking silent event loss; (4) the resolve_dependencies return schema surfaces four distinct states but the proposed Go/WebUI display only accounts for three — dangling dependencies require distinct UI treatment since they demand user action (fixing broken references) rather than passive waiting; (5) no token-budget or summarization strategy exists for the planning prompt's dependency context injection, meaning features with many dependencies and accumulated events risk consuming excessive prompt budget and degrading plan quality."
}
